<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/13521/source/9>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  "“<a href=\"http://www.fcpp.org/publication.php/3348\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\">Conversations from the Frontier</a>,” Frontier Center for Public Policy."^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/13521>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "SC: To be very honest I’m agnostic on this. I don’t have the evidence. I mean I have lots of contradictory evidence but I do think, from my experience on ocean pollution and all the other pollution hypes, that when it goes to the political phase there are huge exaggerations. Once bureaucracies get regulatory and taxation powers, the exaggerations decline, scares may even be forgotten. So I honestly believe that there may be a problem but that this problem also has beneficial sides. We know how positive carbon dioxide is to life. So I do think there’s much exaggeration (of the man-made warming threat), of the negative aspects, for political reasons. So that’s why I’m here (at this Conference). I do think the skeptical scientists are more honest and more truthful than those funded by governments to support the IPCC.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/13521/source/9>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/sonja-boehmer-christiansen/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/sonja-boehmer-christiansen/" .

