<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/12432/source/7>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  "“Is Climate Change Real?” Transcript from “Face The Nation” with Sagarika Ghose. Reproduced by the Liberty Institute, January 20, 2010. *No longer available on live web."^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/12432>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“The climate always changes. The issue is whether it is man induced, anthropogenic global warming or it is a natural cycle of various factors from solar cycles to the ocean temperatures. I think the issue is not so much the science. I think the fundamental problem is the tendency to use science for a particular policy outcome. I think the science is never and answer… . Science cannot give us an answer on anything.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/12432/source/7>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/liberty-institute/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/liberty-institute/" .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/12431/source/6>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  "Roy W. Spencer, Paul K. Driessen, and E. Calvin Beisner. “<a href=\"http://www.cornwallalliance.org/docs/an-examination-of-the-scientific-ethical-and-theological-implications-of-climate-change-policy.pdf\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\">An Examination of the Scientific, Ethical and Theological Implications of Climate Change Policy” (PDF)</a>, Interfaith Stewardship Alliance, 2005. Archived .pdf on file at DeSmog."^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/12431>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“Some people insist that ‘renewable energy’ and ‘sustainable development’ must be the future for Third World countries,” says Barun Mitra, president of the Liberty Institute in Delhi, India. “But poor people need sustained development – not sustainable development. They need to protect their families from real, immediate, life-threatening risks – instead of being condemned to squalor and premature death, to address distant, hypothetical dangers like global warming.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/12431/source/6>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/liberty-institute/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/liberty-institute/" .

