        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  ""^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11092>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“This series shines a light on some of the most dynamic and influential decision makers and intellectuals in Canada and the United States. The episodes present in-depth interviews with such figures as the Honourable Joe Oliver, Canada’s Minister of Natural Resources at the time of the interview; Pierre Desrochers, professor in the geography department at the University of Toronto Mississauga; and Rick George, a partner of the Novo Investment Group and the former CEO of Suncor Energy.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11092/source/>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/montreal-economic-institute/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/montreal-economic-institute/" .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11091/source/>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  ""^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11091>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“In 2013, we pursued our partnership with The World Show on American PBS’s network of affiliated television stations. Having collaborated on the ‘Free Markets Series,’ we are collaborating this time on a television series dealing with energy questions. This series arrives at a crucial time since important decisions on energy-related matters are on the agenda and decision makers are in the process of deploying their strategies. It is imperative that we contribute to this debate through our research and our educational activities.";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11091/source/>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/montreal-economic-institute/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/montreal-economic-institute/" .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11090/source/105>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  "<a href=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20230207193250/https://www.iedm.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/rapport2013_en.pdf\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\">“Ideas for a More Prosperous Nation: Annual Report 2013” (PDF)</a>, <em>Montreal Economic Institute</em>. Archived .pdf on file at DeSmog."^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11090>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“In 2013, we took part in a concrete manner in debates on, among other things, the transportation of oil by pipeline, the rising costs of electricity in Quebec and the management of our forests.” She added,” We even participated in the launch of a new interview series on the American PBS network featuring internationally renowned experts on energy questions.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11090/source/105>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/montreal-economic-institute/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/montreal-economic-institute/" .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11089/source/>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  ""^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11089>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“Climate change has both negative and positive effects. Global warming of less than 2°C, as expected between now and the end of the century, will have positive net effects due in particular to higher crop yields,” the report claimed.";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11089/source/>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/montreal-economic-institute/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/montreal-economic-institute/" .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11088/source/>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  ""^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11088>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“Our researchers also used the occasion, in interviews and blog posts, to criticize the subsidies granted by the City of Montreal to the Formula E race, another ineffective and costly government intervention aiming to promote electric cars. One of the first actions of Montreal’s new municipal government, in the days following its election, was to cancel the event.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11088/source/>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/montreal-economic-institute/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/montreal-economic-institute/" .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11087/source/>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  ""^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11087>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“The first showed that the subsidies handed out by the Quebec and Ontario governments for the purchase of such vehicles are a waste of public funds, and that they are the least efficient and most expensive way of reducing greenhouse gases. We stepped up to the plate again a few weeks later by explaining why the electric vehicle sales quotas imposed on manufacturers are going to end up increasing the cost of conventional vehicles, which is equivalent to a new tax for consumers.";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11087/source/>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/montreal-economic-institute/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/montreal-economic-institute/" .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11086/source/19>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  "<a href=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20220108023920/https://www.iedm.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/rapport2015_en.pdf\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\">“Annual Report 2015” (PDF)</a>, <em>Montreal Economic Institute</em>. Archived .pdf on file at DeSmog."^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11086>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“The issues of energy development and the fight against climate change made headlines once again this year. As in previous years, the MEI emphasized a realistic, pragmatic approach, opposed to that of environmentalist groups that attempt to block any type of development. Canadians will continue to consume hydrocarbons for several decades to come. And it is important that transition measures toward greener energy sources don’t simply end up impoverishing the population, without having any notable effects on the production of greenhouse gases.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/11086/source/19>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/montreal-economic-institute/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
