<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/10765>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "The last component is the political arena and that is where this issue will be finally resolved. Although our preference would be for the issue to simply go away, that is not going to happen. There are too m any special interest groups involved. Most investor-owned utilities are looking for certainty and will merely pass on the costs of a mandatory program. An army of scientists and researchers are determined to make man-made global warming a threat in order to continue obtaining their funding. Federal spending on climate research has increased from a few hundred million dollars prior to 1990 to $1.7 billion dollars today. Publicity on the issue might resurrect a political career. Legislation concerning major issues like global warming will generate tens of millions of dollars in political contributions. Millions will also be donated to environmental groups. Aaron Wildavsky, Professor of Political Science at Berkeley, stated that “global warming” is the mother of all environmental scares. His view is worth quoting, “Warming (and warming alone) through its primary antidote of withdrawing carbon from production and consumption is capable of realizing the environmentalist’s dream of an egalitarian society based on rejection of economic growth in favor of a smaller population, eating lower on the food chain, consuming a lot less, and sharing a much lower level of resources much more&nbsp;equally.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/10765/source/8>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/intermountain-rural-electric-association/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/intermountain-rural-electric-association/" .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/10764/source/8>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  "<a href=\"http://web.archive.org/web/20170719205221/http://cdn.publicinterestnetwork.org/assets/wbI-yjBeoxBc6AImsTQOeg/IREAletter.pdf\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\">IREA Letter&nbsp;(PDF)</a>, July 17, 2006. retrieved from The Public Interest Network.<a href=\"https://www.desmog.com/wp-content/uploads/files/IREAletter.pdf\" data-wpel-link=\"internal\"> Archived .pdf on file at DeSmog</a>."^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/10764>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "Although Senators McCain, Lieberman, Jeffords, Bingaman, and Domenici propose a carbon tax or a mandatory market based greenhouse gas regulatory system, there are others in opposition to these proposals. Rep. Barton has questioned data presented by some climatologists and Sen. James Inhofe, Chairman of the Senate Environment and Public Works Committee, has referred to the threat of a catastrophic climate change as “the greatest hoax ever perpetrated on the American&nbsp;people.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/10764/source/8>;
