<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/6536>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“With genuinely well-established scientific theories, ‘consensus’ is not discussed and the concept of consensus is arguably irrelevant. For example, there is no point to discussing a consensus that the Earth orbits the sun, or that the hydrogen molecule has less mass than the nitrogen molecule. While a consensus may arise surrounding a specific scientific hypothesis or theory, the existence of a consensus is not itself the evidence.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/6536/source/>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/judith-curry/>;
