<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/5262>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“All rhetorical whitewashing put aside, Seibt’s concept of nationalism resembles the concept of ‘ethnopluralism’&nbsp;which is the main concept of the ‘Identitarian Movement’, a far-right group from Europe. It is based on the racist conspiracy theory of the “great replacement” by the French far-right academic Renaud Camus. The far-right terrorists who carried out the attacks in Christchurch, New Zealand, and in Halle, Germany, partially based their manifestos on this racist, anti-Semitic and anti-feminist theory which is also popular amongst the politicians, members and supporters of the ‘Alternative for Germany’&nbsp;and other far-right movements in Germany.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/5262/source/15>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/naomi-seibt/>;
