<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/3924>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“Alarmist predictions amount to nothing more than wild guesses,” Smith began. “The ability to predict far into the future is impossible. Anyone stating that they know what the climate will be in 500 years, or even at the end of this century, is not credible.” He concluded his opening statements, claiming that “Much of climate science today appears to be based more on exaggerations, personal agendas, and questionable predictions than on the scientific method.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/3924/source/79>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/lamar-smith/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/lamar-smith/" .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/3923/source/72>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  "Lamar Smith. “<a href=\"http://web.archive.org/web/20170815200904/http://dailysignal.com/2017/07/24/dont-believe-hysteria-carbon-dioxide/\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\">Don’t Believe the Hysteria Over Carbon Dioxide</a>,” <em>The Daily Signal, </em>July 24, 2017. Archived August 15, 2017. Archive.is URL: <a href=\"https://archive.is/1nxm9\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\">https://archive.is/1nxm9</a>"^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/3923>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“The American people should be made aware of both the negative and positive impacts of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere. Without the whole story, how can we expect an objective evaluation of the issues involving climate change?” Smith asked. He added, “A higher concentration of carbon dioxide in our atmosphere would aid photosynthesis, which in turn contributes to increased plant growth.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/3923/source/72>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/lamar-smith/>;
