<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/3203>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“A 30-year-old, two-pack-a-day smoker has a life expectancy of about eight years less than a nonsmoker. As a result, he will likely draw far less money out of Social Security and require far less in way of expensive long-term medical care. When you add this to the cigarette taxes smokers pay, the ‘social costs’ of smoking turn into a fiscal gain,” Kazman wrote.";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/3203/source/39>;
