<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1485/source/104>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  "<a href=\"http://web.archive.org/web/20170127183838/https://s3-us-west-2.amazonaws.com/gios-web-img-docs/docs/gios/Sustainability_Series/2010-2011/RogerPielke10-19.pdf\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\">“The Climate Fix: What Scientists and Politicians Won’t Tell You About Global Warming” (PDF)</a>, Arizona State University, Global Institute of Sustainability. Archived .pdf on file at&nbsp;DeSmog."^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1485>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“Conventional wisdom on how to deal with climate change has failed us, according to Roger Pielke, and it’s time to change course. Using an arithmetic and logical explanation, Pielke explores the problem and practical ways of meeting growing energy demands. In this thought-provoking discussion of the interaction between science and politics, Pielke proposes a means for digging ourselves out of this climate change mess we’ve&nbsp;created.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1485/source/104>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/roger-pielke-jr/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/roger-pielke-jr/" .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1484/source/109>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  "Daniel Sarewitz and Roger Pielke Jr. <a href=\"http://web.archive.org/web/20170128010217/http://sciencepolicy.colorado.edu/admin/publication_files/resource-69-2000.18.pdf\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\">“Breaking the Global-Warming Gridlock” (PDF)</a>,<em> The Atlantic Monthly</em>, July 2000. Retrieved from Centre for Science &amp; Policy Research at the University of Colorado. Archived .pdf on file at&nbsp;DeSmog."^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1484>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“Reframing the climate problem could mobilize this constituency [those subject to the effects of the weather] and revitalize the Framework Convention. The revitalization could concentrate on coordinating disaster relief, debt relief, and development assistance, and on generating and providing information on climate that participating countries could use in order to reduce the&nbsp;vulnerability.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1484/source/109>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/roger-pielke-jr/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/roger-pielke-jr/" .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1483/source/111>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  "Roger A. Pielke Jr. and Daniel Sarewitz. “<a href=\"http://web.archive.org/web/20170128014643/http://sciencepolicy.colorado.edu/admin/publication_files/2000.17.pdf\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\">Anyone for global warming?</a>” <em>The Washington Times</em>, February 2, 2000. Retrieved from Centre for Science &amp; Policy Research at the University of Colorado. Archived .pdf on file at&nbsp;DeSmog."^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1483>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“Understanding the strengths and limits of predictions is important because our sense of certainty about events in the future influences the actions we take today,” &nbsp;Pielke Jr. and Sarewitz write. “For example, predictions of global warming have focused international environmental efforts on reducing greenhouse gas emissions. But future economic trends, geopolitical events, and technological advances — three variables that defy predictive accuracy — will have a much greater impact on emissions than any conceivable international&nbsp;agreements.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1483/source/111>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/roger-pielke-jr/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/roger-pielke-jr/" .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1482/source/15>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  "<a href=\"http://web.archive.org/web/20170127175820/http://scienceandpublicpolicy.org/images/stories/papers/reprint/pielke_epa_committee.pdf\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\">“STATEMENT OF DR. ROGER PIELKE, JR. to the COMMITTEE ON ENVIRONMENT AND PUBLIC WORKS of the UNITED STATES SENATE HEARING on CLIMATE CHANGE: IT’S HAPPENING NOW 18 July 2013” (PDF)</a>, July 19, 2013. Reprinted by Science &amp; &nbsp;Public Policy Institute. Archived .pdf on file at&nbsp;DeSmog."^^rdf:HTML .

