<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1195/source/20>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  "Tom Bawden. “<a href=\"https://www.independent.co.uk/environment/mps-accuse-bbc-of-creating-false-balance-on-climate-change-with-unqualified-sceptics-9231176.html\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\">MPs accuse BBC of creating ‘false balance’ on climate change with unqualified sceptics,</a>” <em>Independent</em>, April 2, 2014. <a href=\"http://web.archive.org/web/20181228005601/https://www.independent.co.uk/environment/mps-accuse-bbc-of-creating-false-balance-on-climate-change-with-unqualified-sceptics-9231176.html\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\">Archived December 28, 2018</a>. Archive.fo URL: <a href=\"https://archive.fo/Wb9mj\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\">https://archive.fo/Wb9mj</a>"^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1195>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“[…] in just one show, [Neil] has done more to promote an active understanding of climate science and its controversies than has been done by the Carbon Brief blog, academics at the Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism and elsewhere, Bad Science warriors, and a legion of Tweeters who claim to speak for science have done in their entire existences. Along the way, it is possible that Neil made some inconsequential technical mistakes. But by contrast, the uncritical reproduction of scientific orthodoxy is a far more egregious error: it denies that error can be observed from without the consensus. So much for ‘science’.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1195/source/20>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/ben-pile/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:inLanguage        "English"^^schema:Text;
        schema:publisher         <http://desmog.com/>;
        schema:url               "https://www.desmog.com/ben-pile/" .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1194/source/19>
        rdf:type         schema:CreativeWork;
        schema:citation  "“<a href=\"http://web.archive.org/web/20181228005413/http://blogs.nottingham.ac.uk/makingsciencepublic/2013/07/23/whats-behind-the-battle-of-received-wisdoms/\" rel=\"nofollow external noopener noreferrer\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\">What’s behind the battle of received wisdoms?</a>” <em>University of Nottingham</em>, July 23, 2013. Archived December 28, 2018. Archive.fo URL:<a href=\"https://archive.fo/j2SOD\" data-wpel-link=\"external\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"external noopener noreferrer\"> https://archive.fo/j2SOD</a>"^^rdf:HTML .

<http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1194>
        rdf:type                 schema:Claim;
        schema:abstract          "“[…] in just one show, [Neil] has done more to promote an active understanding of climate science and its controversies than has been done by the Carbon Brief blog, academics at the Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism and elsewhere, Bad Science warriors, and a legion of Tweeters who claim to speak for science have done in their entire existences. Along the way, it is possible that Neil made some inconsequential technical mistakes. But by contrast, the uncritical reproduction of scientific orthodoxy is a far more egregious error: it denies that error can be observed from without the consensus. So much for ‘science’.”";
        schema:archivedAt        <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/claim/1194/source/19>;
        schema:author            <http://data.open.ac.uk/climate-sense/entity/ben-pile/>;
        schema:claimInterpreter  <http://desmog.com/>;
